# Getting started

## Prerequisites

- [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) (it installs Python {{ python_version }} for you from `.python-version`)
- git
{% if is_service %}
- Docker (optional, for `docker compose up`)
{% endif %}

## Setup

```bash
uv sync                # creates .venv from pyproject.toml / uv.lock
scripts/check.sh       # the quality gate: should pass on a fresh checkout
```

Add dependencies with `uv add <pkg>` (runtime) or `uv add --dev <pkg>` (tooling). Never edit
`uv.lock` by hand.
{% if is_cli %}

## Run

```bash
uv run {{ project_slug }} --help
uv run python -m {{ package_name }} --help
```

The CLI lives in `src/{{ package_name }}/cli.py`. Keep `main(argv)` thin: parse arguments, call
library code, return an exit code.
{% endif %}
{% if is_service %}

## Run the service

```bash
cp .env.example .env
uv run uvicorn {{ package_name }}.infrastructure.api.app:create_app --factory --reload
curl localhost:8000/health

docker compose up --build     # same thing, in a container
```

Configuration comes from environment variables via `pydantic-settings`
(`src/{{ package_name }}/infrastructure/config.py`); see `.env.example` for the keys. Read
[`core_concepts.md`](core_concepts.md) before adding a feature: it explains where code goes.
{% endif %}
{% if is_ds %}

## Data and notebooks

- Raw and derived data live under `data/` (gitignored: it can hold sensitive or large files).
  `{{ package_name }}.paths` gives you the directories and creates them on demand.
- Notebooks live in `notebooks/`. `nbstripout` is installed as a git filter so outputs never get
  committed. Move anything reusable out of a notebook into `src/{{ package_name }}/`, where it is
  typed and tested.
- Stages: `ingest/` (get data in), `features/` (transform), `models/` (fit and evaluate).
- Only synthetic fixtures under `tests/fixtures/` are tracked. Check file contents before
  committing any data file.

```bash
uv run jupyter lab      # after: uv add --dev jupyterlab
```
{% endif %}

## Development workflow

1. Plan: `scripts/new_feature.sh <name>`, fill in the plan, get it reviewed.
2. Build on a `feature/<name>` branch with tests alongside the code.
3. `scripts/check.sh` before every commit. Commit often; git is how we roll back and forward.
4. Update `docs/` and `STATUS.md`, then archive the plan from `dev/active/` to `dev/archive/`.

See [`dev/README.md`](../../dev/README.md) for details.
